44 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 146 0 R/E(A model that performs speech recognition may run on a low-end smartphone, although access to the power of cloud computing may be needed to train it. )/Pg 8 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

45 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 147 0 R/E(A common approach used when the host device is not connected to the internet is to train the model in the cloud and then deploy it to the host device.)/Pg 8 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

46 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 148 0 R/E(ML typically benefits from hardware that supports the following attributes:)/Pg 8 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

47 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 149 0 R/E(Low-precision arithmetic: This uses fewer bits for computation \(e.g., 8 instead of 32 bits, which is usually all that is needed for ML\).)/Pg 8 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

48 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 150 0 R/E(The ability to work with large data structures \(e.g., to support matrix multiplication\).)/Pg 8 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

49 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 151 0 R/E(Massively parallel \(concurrent\) processing)/Pg 8 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

50 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 152 0 R/E(General-purpose CPUs provide support for complex operations that are not typically required for ML applications and only provide a few cores. )/Pg 8 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

51 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 153 0 R/E(As a result, their architecture is less efficient for training and running ML models when compared to GPUs, which have thousands of cores and which are designed to perform the massively parallel but relatively simple processing of images. )/Pg 8 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

52 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 154 0 R/E(As a consequence, GPUs typically outperform CPUs for ML applications, even though CPUs typically have faster clock speeds. )/Pg 8 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

53 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 155 0 R/E(For small-scale ML work, GPUs generally offer the best option.)/Pg 8 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

